is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc11, pc12
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Layer-3 host addressing on the Location C access segment (192.168.200.0/24) causing ARP instability on switch5.

Mechanism: Two different NICs are claiming the same IP address, so neighbors and the switch keep relearning 192.168.200.12’s MAC via ARP from alternating sources. Hosts direct traffic to whichever MAC last answered ARP; when that isn’t the intended endpoint, packets are effectively black-holed, producing intermittent reachability.

Localization: PC11 (cd_sw5_fa1_1, eth0 MAC b6:7b:51:4c:6e:18) and PC12 (cd_sw5_fa2_1, eth0 MAC 36:a4:6a:e6:4f:cb) are both configured with IP 192.168.200.12/24 and default gateway 192.168.200.1 (evidence: get_host_net_config on pc11 and pc12). The topology-wide probe also shows both pc11 and pc12 mapping to 192.168.200.12. This exactly matches the reported symptom of the switch’s ARP entry for 192.168.200.12 flipping between two MACs.

Root cause: Duplicate IP assignment on the access LAN—pc11 and pc12 are simultaneously configured with 192.168.200.12.

Corrective fix:
- Assign unique IPs to these hosts so only one device owns 192.168.200.12; change the other to a free address in 192.168.200.0/24 (e.g., the host actually intended to be “PC11” should use its own unique .11 address if that’s the site convention).
- After correcting addressing, clear stale ARP entries on nearby devices (or wait ARP timeout); optionally send gratuitous ARPs from the correctly addressed hosts.
- If using DHCP, fix reservations/scope to prevent duplicate leases; if using static IPs, update documentation and consider enabling duplicate address detection/ARP inspection on the switch.

Once unique addressing is enforced, ARP will stabilize and connectivity will be consistent. 
=== END ===